topic("budgeting", "Income, budgeting & goals", ["Financial Analysis"],
      "Where money comes from, how to read a pay stub, how to set goals and build a budget, net worth, records and charitable giving.")

sub("income-paystub", "Sources of income and reading a pay stub", ["FI:061", "FI:068"],
    ["Income comes from work (wages, salary, commissions, tips, bonuses), from assets (interest, dividends, rent, capital gains), from owning a business (profit), and from government transfers (Social Security, unemployment benefits). Total compensation is more than the paycheck: employer health insurance, retirement matches, paid time off and stock awards can add 30% or more to the value of a job.",
     "A pay stub shows gross pay (before deductions) and net pay (what lands in the bank). In between are mandatory deductions (federal income tax withholding based on your W-4, Social Security and Medicare, together called FICA, and state or local income tax where it applies) and voluntary ones (401(k) contributions, health premiums, HSA contributions). Year-to-date totals help you check that withholding is on track."],
    ["Pay frequency: weekly (52 checks), biweekly (26), semimonthly (24), monthly (12). Biweekly workers get two 'extra' paychecks a year.",
     "Social Security tax: 6.2% on wages up to the wage base; Medicare: 1.45% on all wages, plus 0.9% on wages over $200,000 (single).",
     "Pre-tax deductions (traditional 401(k), most health premiums, HSA) lower taxable income; Roth 401(k) contributions don't.",
     "Form W-4 tells the employer how much federal tax to withhold; update it after marriage, a new child or a second job.",
     "Salaried exempt employees don't get overtime; hourly non-exempt workers get 1.5 times pay over 40 hours a week (Fair Labor Standards Act)."],
    ["The Social Security wage base is $184,500 for 2026 and $192,300 for 2027 (Social Security Administration, announced Oct. 2026).",
     "The federal minimum wage has been $7.25 an hour since 2009; many states and cities set higher minimums (U.S. Department of Labor).",
     "For 2025 through 2028, workers can deduct up to $25,000 of qualified tips and up to $12,500 of overtime premium pay ($25,000 joint) from federal income tax; payroll taxes still apply (IRS, 2025 tax law)."],
    ["A server earning $40,000 including $15,000 in tips can now deduct those tips from federal taxable income, but still pays Social Security and Medicare on them.",
     "Many employers offer earned wage access apps (like DailyPay) that let workers get paid before payday, sometimes for a fee."],
    ["Net pay is after deductions; gross pay is before.", "FICA = Social Security + Medicare, not federal income tax."],
    "\"Your $52,000 salary isn't $1,000 a week in your account. After taxes and a 6% 401(k) contribution, expect roughly $720 to $780 a week depending on your state, so we budget from net pay.\"",
    [("Why is our client's paycheck smaller than they expected?",
      "Because deductions come out before the money arrives. Federal and state income tax withholding, 6.2% for Social Security, 1.45% for Medicare, plus health insurance and 401(k) contributions can take 20% to 30% of gross pay. A $4,000 monthly salary might deposit around $3,000. We'd walk through the pay stub line by line, check the W-4 is set correctly, and build the budget from net pay so there are no surprises."),
     ("Should our client take the job with the higher salary or the one with better benefits?",
      "Compare total compensation, not just salary. A 5% 401(k) match on a $60,000 salary is $3,000 a year of free money, and employer health coverage can be worth $7,000 or more to a single worker. A $62,000 job with no match and expensive insurance can be worth less than a $58,000 job with strong benefits. We'd put both offers side by side in a simple table, which is also a great visual for this kind of decision.")],
    [IRS, ["SSA", "https://www.ssa.gov"], ["U.S. Department of Labor", "https://www.dol.gov"]])

sub("goals", "Setting financial goals and making decisions", ["FI:065", "FI:783"],
    ["A financial goal is a specific thing you want your money to do. Good goals are SMART: specific, measurable, attainable, relevant and time-bound. 'Save more' is a wish; 'save $6,000 for an emergency fund by next December by moving $500 a month' is a goal. Goals are usually grouped by time: short-term (under a year), medium-term (one to five years) and long-term (five years or more). The time frame decides where the money should go.",
     "Responsible decisions follow a process: define the problem, gather information, list alternatives, weigh each one's costs and benefits (including opportunity cost, what you give up), choose, act and review. Behavioral finance shows that people procrastinate, overweight today versus the future and follow the crowd. Good plans work with human nature by automating saving and making the right choice the default."],
    ["Common order of priorities: starter emergency fund, get the full employer match, pay off high-interest debt, full emergency fund, retirement saving of 15% of income, other goals.",
     "Short-term money goes in savings or CDs; long-term money can be invested in stocks.",
     "Opportunity cost: $5 a day on coffee is about $1,800 a year, which could fund a Roth IRA contribution.",
     "Needs vs wants: a need keeps you safe, housed, fed, healthy and employed; everything else is a want.",
     "Review goals at life events: new job, marriage, a child, a move."],
    ["People who automate savings save more: auto-enrollment raised 401(k) participation to around 90% of eligible new hires in plans that use it, compared with much lower rates when workers must opt in (Vanguard How America Saves).",
     "In the Fed's 2024 survey, 63% of adults said they would cover a $400 emergency expense with cash or its equivalent (Federal Reserve SHED report)."],
    ["Fidelity, Schwab and Vanguard let customers set automatic monthly investments, the 'pay yourself first' habit in practice.",
     "Apps like YNAB and Monarch Money let couples set shared goals and track progress in real time."],
    ["SMART goals must have a deadline (time-bound).", "Opportunity cost is the value of the next-best alternative, not the total of all alternatives."],
    "\"Let's turn 'save more' into a SMART goal: $6,000 in an emergency fund by December 2027, $500 a month, moved automatically the day after payday.\"",
    [("How will our client stay motivated to stick with this plan?",
      "By making it automatic and making progress visible. We'd schedule transfers the day after payday so saving happens before they can spend it. That's how auto-enrollment pushed 401(k) participation to about 90% in plans that use it. Then they'd see a simple progress bar toward each goal and get a quarterly check-in with us. Small wins, like hitting the first $1,000, keep people going."),
     ("What should our client do first: pay off debt or start investing?",
      "Both, in a specific order. First a starter emergency fund of about $1,000 so a surprise doesn't go on a credit card. Next, contribute enough to get the full employer match, because a 50% match is an instant 50% return. Then attack any debt above about 8%, like a 22% credit card, before investing more. That order gives the highest guaranteed return on every dollar.")],
    [CFPB, NGPF])

sub("budget", "Building a personal budget that works", ["FI:066", "FI:106"],
    ["A budget is a plan for how you'll spend and save your income over a period, usually a month. It starts with net income, then lists fixed expenses (rent, insurance, loan payments), variable expenses (groceries, gas, entertainment) and savings goals. Comparing what you planned with what actually happened, a variance, shows where to adjust. A budget doesn't limit freedom; it decides ahead of time where money goes, so it isn't wasted.",
     "There are several methods. The 50/30/20 rule puts about 50% of after-tax income toward needs, 30% toward wants and 20% toward savings and extra debt payments. Zero-based budgeting gives every dollar a job until income minus planned spending equals zero. The envelope method (now often digital) caps spending by category. 'Pay yourself first' moves savings out before anything else. The best method is the one the client will actually follow."],
    ["Step 1: find monthly net income (use the lowest typical month if income varies).",
     "Step 2: list fixed costs, then average the variable ones from the last three months of statements.",
     "Step 3: set savings goals as a 'bill' that's paid first.",
     "Step 4: track spending weekly with an app or a spreadsheet.",
     "Step 5: review monthly, compare budget to actual, and adjust categories that keep going over.",
     "Irregular income (gig work, commissions): budget from a base month and put windfalls in savings first."],
    ["Housing is the largest household cost; it took about a third of average consumer spending in 2024 (BLS Consumer Expenditure Survey).",
     "The 50/30/20 rule was popularized by Elizabeth Warren and Amelia Warren Tyagi in the 2005 book All Your Worth."],
    ["Apps like Monarch Money, YNAB and Rocket Money link to bank accounts and categorize spending automatically.",
     "Many people cancel forgotten subscriptions after reviewing bank statements; the FTC's 2024 'click to cancel' rule was struck down by a federal appeals court in 2025, so cancelling can still take effort."],
    ["A budget is a plan; a financial statement records what already happened.", "Fixed expenses stay the same each month; variable ones change."],
    "\"Using 50/30/20 on your $3,000 monthly take-home pay: $1,500 for needs, $900 for wants, and $600 a month to savings and debt, which is $7,200 a year working for you.\"",
    [("Why does a family that earns good money still need a budget?",
      "Because income doesn't guarantee savings; spending tends to rise with income. A budget makes sure the family's priorities, like an emergency fund and college for their kids, get funded first instead of whatever is left over. With 50/30/20, a family taking home $8,000 a month would send $1,600 to savings automatically. The budget is what turns a good income into real wealth."),
     ("Our client's income changes every month. How can they budget?",
      "Budget from a base month, the lowest typical month of income, and cover needs and minimum savings from that. In good months, the extra goes first to an income-smoothing buffer in savings, then to goals. For example, a rideshare driver who earns between $3,000 and $5,000 budgets on $3,000 and banks the rest. The buffer pays them in slow months, so the budget never breaks.")],
    [CFPB, NGPF])

sub("networth-records", "Net worth and keeping financial records", ["FI:562", "FI:069"],
    ["Net worth is what you own minus what you owe: assets minus liabilities. Assets include cash, investments, retirement accounts, a home and a car (at what it could sell for today). Liabilities include credit card balances, student loans, car loans and a mortgage. It's a personal balance sheet. Net worth can be negative for a young graduate with student loans, and that's normal. The trend matters more than the number.",
     "Financial records are the paper trail that lets you budget, file taxes, prove ownership, file insurance claims and catch errors. Good record-keeping means knowing what to keep, for how long, and where it's stored safely, whether in a fireproof box, a secure cloud folder or both, with a trusted person who knows where to find it."],
    ["Net worth = total assets - total liabilities. Calculate it once or twice a year.",
     "Liquid assets (cash, savings) can be spent quickly; illiquid assets (a home, a retirement account) cannot without cost or delay.",
     "Keep tax returns and supporting documents at least 3 years (longer in some cases, e.g. 6 years if income was underreported by more than 25%) (IRS).",
     "Keep home purchase and improvement records until you sell, plus 3 years, to prove cost basis.",
     "Keep pay stubs until you match them to the year's W-2; shred anything with an account or Social Security number when you no longer need it."],
    ["Median U.S. family net worth was $192,900 in 2022, but the average was about $1.06 million, because a few very wealthy families pull the average up (Federal Reserve Survey of Consumer Finances).",
     "The IRS generally has 3 years from the filing date to audit a return, which is why 3 years is the basic rule for keeping records."],
    ["After hurricanes and wildfires, families with photos of their belongings and digital copies of insurance policies settle claims faster (Insurance Information Institute).",
     "Apps like Empower show net worth automatically by linking all accounts."],
    ["Median and average net worth are very different; use the median for a 'typical' family.", "A car is an asset at its current resale value, not the purchase price."],
    "\"Your net worth today is negative $18,000, which is normal with student loans. Our goal is to make it positive within three years, and you'll see it climb every quarter.\"",
    [("Why does our client's net worth matter if they're paying all their bills?",
      "Because paying bills shows cash flow, but net worth shows progress. A family can pay every bill and still go backward if debt grows faster than savings. Tracking net worth twice a year shows whether the plan is working. If it rises from $40,000 to $55,000 in a year, we know saving and debt payoff are on track."),
     ("What records should our client keep, and for how long?",
      "Tax returns and supporting documents for at least three years, because that's generally how long the IRS has to audit. Home purchase and improvement records until the home is sold, plus three years. Insurance policies and a photo inventory of belongings for as long as they're in force. We'd scan everything into a secure, encrypted cloud folder and shred the paper copies with account numbers.")],
    [IRS, ["Fed Survey of Consumer Finances", "https://www.federalreserve.gov/econres/scfindex.htm"]])

sub("giving", "Charitable giving", ["FI:567"],
    ["Charitable giving is donating money, property or time to nonprofit organizations. For individuals it reflects values and can lower taxes; for businesses it builds reputation, employee pride and community ties. A donation is tax-deductible only if it goes to a qualified organization, usually a 501(c)(3) public charity, and you follow the record-keeping rules.",
     "Smart giving plans ahead. Donating appreciated stock instead of cash avoids capital gains tax. A donor-advised fund lets someone take a deduction now and choose charities over time. People 70½ and older can give directly from an IRA (a qualified charitable distribution), which doesn't count as taxable income. Before giving, check that a charity is legitimate and uses money well."],
    ["Verify a charity with the IRS Tax Exempt Organization Search, and review ratings on Charity Navigator or BBB Wise Giving Alliance.",
     "Get a written acknowledgment for any single gift of $250 or more (IRS).",
     "Starting in 2026, taxpayers who don't itemize can deduct up to $1,000 ($2,000 for married couples) of cash gifts to charity (IRS, 2025 tax law).",
     "Donating stock you've held more than a year: deduct its full market value and pay no capital gains tax on the growth.",
     "Businesses: corporate giving, employee matching gifts, volunteer days and cause marketing."],
    ["Americans gave about $592.5 billion to charity in 2024, and about two-thirds came from individuals (Giving USA 2025).",
     "The $1,000/$2,000 deduction for non-itemizers begins in tax year 2026 (IRS)."],
    ["After disasters, scammers create fake relief charities; the FTC advises giving to established organizations and never paying by gift card or wire.",
     "Many employers, such as Google and Microsoft, match employee donations, which can double a gift."],
    ["Gifts to individuals (like a GoFundMe for a friend) are generally not tax-deductible.", "You need a receipt for each gift of $250 or more."],
    "\"Giving can be part of the plan: $100 a month to a cause you care about, and starting in 2026 you can deduct up to $1,000 of cash gifts even without itemizing.\"",
    [("Our client wants to give back but money is tight. What would you suggest?",
      "Start small and automatic, and give time too. Even $25 a month to a verified 501(c)(3) builds the habit, and starting in 2026 up to $1,000 of cash gifts is deductible without itemizing. If the employer matches donations, that $25 becomes $50. Volunteering costs nothing and builds connections. Giving fits inside the budget as a planned line, so it never competes with the emergency fund."),
     ("How can our client make sure a charity is legitimate?",
      "Check it before giving. Search the IRS Tax Exempt Organization database to confirm 501(c)(3) status, then look at Charity Navigator for how much goes to programs versus overhead. Never give by gift card, wire or crypto to someone who contacts you, which the FTC says are signs of a scam. A few minutes of checking makes sure the money actually helps.")],
    [IRS, ["Charity Navigator", "https://www.charitynavigator.org"]])

# =====================================================================
topic("investing", "Saving & investing", ["Financial Analysis"],
      "Why to save and invest, the time value of money, every major investment type, risk and diversification, and how to read securities information.")

sub("why-invest", "Why save and invest", ["FI:270"],
    ["Saving means setting money aside somewhere safe and easy to reach, like a savings account, for emergencies and near-term goals. Investing means buying assets, like stocks, bonds or real estate, that can grow over time but can also lose value. Both matter. Saving protects you from setbacks; investing is how money outgrows inflation and builds wealth for long-term goals like retirement.",
     "The key reason to invest is compounding: earnings start earning their own returns. Over decades, time does more of the work than the amount you put in. Inflation is the hidden reason not to sit in cash: at 3% inflation, prices double in about 24 years, so money that earns nothing loses half its buying power. Investing also funds the economy, because the money people invest becomes capital that businesses use to grow."],
    ["Emergency fund first (3-6 months of expenses), then invest for goals 5+ years away.",
     "Match the account to the timeline: under 1 year savings; 1-5 years CDs, Treasuries or a conservative mix; 5+ years diversified stock funds.",
     "Use tax-advantaged accounts first: 401(k) match, Roth IRA, HSA.",
     "Automate: invest a fixed amount every payday (dollar-cost averaging).",
     "Stay invested through downturns; missing the market's best days sharply cuts long-term returns."],
    ["From 1928 through 2025 U.S. stocks returned about 10% a year on average, 3-month Treasury bills about 3.3% and 10-year Treasury bonds about 4.5% (NYU Stern, Damodaran historical data).",
     "$200 a month from age 22 to 65 at 8% grows to about $895,000; starting at 32 gives about $387,000 (future-value calculation).",
     "Top high-yield savings accounts paid about 4.35% APY in mid-November 2026, while many big-bank savings accounts pay far less."],
    ["An investor who put money in the S&P 500 at its 2007 peak and held on was back to even by early 2013 and far ahead by 2020.",
     "Fidelity lets customers buy fractional shares for as little as $1 (Schwab from $5), so even a teenager can own part of an S&P 500 fund."],
    ["Saving is for safety and liquidity; investing is for growth with risk.", "Past average returns are not guaranteed future returns; never present them as a promise."],
    "\"Saving keeps you safe; investing makes you grow. We'd do both: an emergency fund first, then automatic monthly investing into a low-cost index fund for anything five years out.\"",
    [("Why should a 22-year-old invest now instead of waiting until they earn more?",
      "Because time is worth more than money when you're young. $200 a month from 22 to 65 at an 8% average grows to about $895,000, but starting at 32 grows to about $387,000, less than half, even though only ten years were skipped. Waiting costs one full doubling of the money. Starting small now, even $50 a month, beats starting big later."),
     ("What if the market crashes right after our client invests?",
      "It probably will drop at some point, and that's OK for long-term money. The S&P 500 fell about 37% in 2008 and 18% in 2022 but recovered and reached new highs both times. Because the client invests a fixed amount every month, a drop means buying more shares at lower prices. That's why we only invest money they won't need for five or more years, and the emergency fund stays in insured savings."),
     ("Why not just keep everything in a high-yield savings account at 4%?",
      "It's perfect for the emergency fund, but too conservative for 30-year goals. Savings rates move with the Fed and fell to nearly zero from 2020 to 2022. Over long periods, stocks have averaged about 10% versus about 3.3% for Treasury bills. On $10,000 over 30 years, that's roughly $174,000 versus $26,000. We'd use both: savings for safety and investments for growth.")],
    [INV, ["Investor.gov compound interest calculator", "https://www.investor.gov/financial-tools-calculators/calculators/compound-interest-calculator"]])

sub("tvm", "Time value of money (concept and calculations)", ["FI:062", "FI:238"],
    ["The time value of money says a dollar today is worth more than a dollar in the future, because today's dollar can be invested to earn interest, and because inflation and uncertainty make future dollars less valuable. Future value (FV) is what money grows to; present value (PV) is what a future amount is worth today. Moving money forward in time is compounding; moving it back is discounting.",
     "Four things drive every time-value problem: the amount, the interest rate, the number of periods and how often interest compounds. The same ideas price bonds, value businesses, compare loan offers and decide whether to take a lump sum or annual payments. On the DECA exam you'll use a four-function calculator, so know the formulas and how to multiply step by step."],
    ["Future value: FV = PV x (1 + r)^n. $1,000 at 5% for 10 years = $1,628.89.",
     "Present value: PV = FV / (1 + r)^n. $10,000 needed in 10 years at 6% is worth $5,583.95 today.",
     "Future value of regular deposits (annuity): FV = PMT x [((1 + r)^n - 1) / r]. $100 a month at 6% (0.5% a month) for 30 years = about $100,452.",
     "More frequent compounding grows money faster; APY shows the effect for a year (a 5% APR compounded monthly is a 5.12% APY).",
     "Rule of 72: years to double = 72 / rate. At 6%, about 12 years.",
     "Simple interest: I = P x r x t (no compounding), used for some short-term loans."],
    ["$100 a month for 30 years is $36,000 deposited; at 6% it grows to about $100,452, so compounding adds about $64,000.",
     "Lottery winners often choose a lump sum that is roughly half the advertised annuity jackpot, a real time-value trade-off (state lottery disclosures)."],
    ["Lotteries advertise the annuity value; the cash option is the present value of those payments.",
     "Car dealers who offer '0% financing or $3,000 cash back' are offering a time-value choice: compare the total cost of each option."],
    ["Present value gets smaller as the interest rate or the time gets bigger.", "Use the rate per period: a 6% annual rate compounded monthly is 0.5% per month for 12n periods."],
    "\"Because of the time value of money, the $5,000 you invest this year is worth more than $10,000 invested ten years from now at the same return, after you account for growth.\"",
    [("Should our client take a $500,000 lump sum now or $40,000 a year for 20 years?",
      "Compare present values. $40,000 a year for 20 years adds up to $800,000, but at a 6% discount rate those payments are worth about $459,000 today, less than the $500,000 lump sum. If the client can invest with discipline, the lump sum wins on the math. If they'd likely overspend, the annuity's built-in discipline might be worth more. We'd show both numbers and let their habits decide."),
     ("How would you explain compounding to a client who isn't good with numbers?",
      "It's like a snowball rolling downhill: it picks up more snow because it's already big. Your money earns returns, then those returns earn returns. $100 a month for 30 years is $36,000 of deposits, but at 6% it grows to about $100,000. Two-thirds of the ending balance is growth, not money you put in. The earlier the snowball starts, the bigger it gets.")],
    [INV])

sub("inv-types", "Types of investments", ["FI:077"],
    ["Investments fall into a few big families. Stocks (equities) are ownership in a company; you profit from price growth and dividends, with higher risk. Bonds (fixed income) are loans to a government or company that pay interest and return principal at maturity; they're usually steadier than stocks. Cash equivalents, such as savings, CDs, money market funds and Treasury bills, are the safest and lowest-returning.",
     "Pooled funds make diversification easy. Mutual funds and exchange-traded funds (ETFs) hold hundreds or thousands of securities; index funds simply copy a market index like the S&P 500 at very low cost. Beyond these are real estate (directly or through REITs), commodities, annuities, and higher-risk assets like options, futures and cryptocurrency. Each trades off return, risk and liquidity differently."],
    ["Common stock: voting rights, dividends not guaranteed. Preferred stock: fixed dividend paid before common shareholders, usually no vote.",
     "Bonds: Treasury (backed by the U.S. government), municipal (interest often free of federal tax), corporate (higher yield, more credit risk). Credit ratings run from AAA down; below BBB- is 'junk' or high-yield.",
     "Mutual fund: priced once a day at net asset value (NAV). ETF: trades all day like a stock, usually with lower costs and better tax efficiency.",
     "Index fund: tracks an index; low fees. Actively managed fund: a manager picks investments; higher fees.",
     "CD: fixed rate for a fixed term, FDIC-insured, penalty for early withdrawal. Treasury bill: 4 to 52 weeks, sold at a discount, interest free of state tax.",
     "REIT: owns income-producing real estate and must pay out at least 90% of taxable income as dividends.",
     "Annuity: an insurance contract that can turn savings into guaranteed income; often has high fees and surrender charges."],
    ["Broad index funds commonly charge expense ratios under 0.10% a year, and Fidelity offers ZERO index funds at 0% (fund disclosures).",
     "Roughly 9 in 10 actively managed U.S. large-cap stock funds trailed the S&P 500 over 15 years (S&P Dow Jones Indices SPIVA report).",
     "The SEC approved the first spot bitcoin ETFs in January 2024 (SEC)."],
    ["Vanguard founder John Bogle launched the first index mutual fund for individual investors in 1976.",
     "Spot bitcoin ETFs gathered tens of billions of dollars in their first year, but bitcoin's price fell by more than half in 2022, a reminder of crypto's risk."],
    ["Bondholders are lenders; stockholders are owners.", "Money market funds are investments (not FDIC-insured); money market deposit accounts at banks are insured."],
    "\"For a first investor, one low-cost total-market index fund or a target-date fund gives you thousands of companies in a single purchase for less than 0.1% a year.\"",
    [("What's the difference between a stock and a bond, in one sentence each?",
      "A stock makes you a part-owner of the company, so you share in its growth and its risk. A bond makes you a lender: the company or government pays you interest and returns your money at maturity. Stocks have averaged about 10% a year over the long run with big swings, and Treasury bonds about 4.5% with smaller ones. That's why a young client holds mostly stocks and shifts toward bonds as goals get closer."),
     ("Why recommend an index fund instead of picking individual stocks?",
      "Because it's cheaper, more diversified and, over time, usually wins. An S&P 500 index fund owns 500 companies for a fee under 0.1%, and the SPIVA report shows about 9 in 10 active large-cap funds trailed the index over 15 years. One company can go to zero, like Enron or Lehman Brothers, but a whole index rarely does. For most clients, an index fund is the simplest way to own the market's growth."),
     ("Should our client invest in cryptocurrency?",
      "Only a small amount, and only money they can afford to lose. Crypto isn't FDIC- or SIPC-insured against losses, and bitcoin fell more than 60% in 2022 before recovering. If they're curious, a spot bitcoin ETF at a regulated brokerage, capped at around 5% of investments, limits the damage if it goes wrong. The core plan stays in diversified index funds.")],
    [INV, FINRA])

sub("risk-diversify", "Risk, return, diversification and asset allocation", ["FI:077", "FI:270", "FI:574"],
    ["Every investment balances risk and return: to get higher expected returns, you accept more ups and downs and a chance of loss. Risk comes in several forms: market risk (the whole market falls), interest-rate risk (bond prices fall when rates rise), inflation risk (returns don't keep up with prices), credit or default risk (the borrower doesn't pay), liquidity risk (you can't sell quickly at a fair price), business risk (one company fails) and currency risk (foreign investments lose value when the dollar strengthens).",
     "Diversification, spreading money across many investments that don't move together, reduces risk without giving up much return. Asset allocation, the mix of stocks, bonds and cash, is the biggest driver of a portfolio's behavior. It should fit the client's time horizon and risk tolerance. Rebalancing, moving back to the target mix once or twice a year, keeps risk from drifting."],
    ["Risk tolerance: how much loss the client can stand emotionally. Risk capacity: how much loss they can afford financially. Plan to the lower of the two.",
     "Time horizon: the longer the goal, the more stock risk the client can take, because there's time to recover.",
     "A rough rule of thumb: hold about '110 minus your age' percent in stocks; target-date funds automate a similar glide path.",
     "Diversify across companies, industries, countries and asset classes; avoid concentrating in your employer's stock.",
     "Rebalance by selling what grew and buying what lagged; doing it in retirement accounts avoids taxes."],
    ["In 2022 both stocks and bonds fell: the S&P 500 lost about 18% and the Bloomberg U.S. Aggregate Bond Index about 13%, a rare year when diversification across those two didn't help.",
     "Enron employees held a large share of their 401(k)s in Enron stock; when it collapsed in 2001, many lost most of their retirement savings."],
    ["Bed Bath & Beyond, once a market favorite, went bankrupt in 2023; investors who owned it inside an index fund barely noticed, while those who owned only that stock lost everything.",
     "Lehman Brothers went from investment bank to bankruptcy in 2008; diversified investors recovered with the market."],
    ["Diversification lowers company-specific risk; it can't remove market risk.", "Higher risk means a higher expected return, not a guaranteed one."],
    "\"We won't bet your future on one company. A diversified fund spreads your money across thousands, so if one fails, like Bed Bath & Beyond in 2023, you barely feel it.\"",
    [("How do you decide how much risk our client should take?",
      "We look at two things: their time horizon and how they'd react to a drop. A 25-year-old investing for retirement has 40 years to recover, so 80% to 90% in stocks makes sense. Money for a house in three years belongs mostly in CDs or Treasuries. We'd also ask, 'If your $10,000 fell to $7,000 in a bad year, would you sell?' If yes, we dial the stock share down, because the best plan is the one they'll stick with."),
     ("Our client wants to put everything into their employer's stock because the company is doing well. Thoughts?",
      "We'd advise against putting more than about 10% in any one stock, especially your employer's. If the company struggles, you could lose your job and your savings at the same time. That happened to Enron employees in 2001. Keep the employer stock you get as a match, and put new money into a diversified index fund. It's about not doubling up on one risk.")],
    [INV, FINRA])

sub("securities-info", "Sources of securities information and reading a securities table", ["FI:274", "FI:275"],
    ["Investors need reliable information to make decisions. The most important primary sources are the documents companies must file with the SEC on its free EDGAR system: the 10-K (annual report with audited financial statements), the 10-Q (quarterly report), the 8-K (major events, like a CEO leaving or a merger) and the proxy statement (executive pay and board elections). A fund's prospectus explains its goals, risks and fees. Secondary sources include financial news, brokerage research, Morningstar ratings and stock screeners.",
     "A securities table or stock quote summarizes trading: ticker symbol, last price, change, volume, the day's high and low, the 52-week range, dividend and yield, the price-to-earnings (P/E) ratio and market capitalization. Reading it helps judge whether a stock is cheap or expensive relative to its earnings, how much income it pays and how volatile it's been."],
    ["Dividend yield = annual dividend / price. A $1.00 dividend on a $50 stock is a 2% yield.",
     "P/E ratio = price / earnings per share. $50 / $2.50 EPS = 20 times earnings.",
     "Market cap = share price x shares outstanding (large-cap generally above $10 billion).",
     "Volume = shares traded that day; unusual volume often signals news.",
     "Bond prices are quoted as a percent of face value: a quote of 98.5 on a $1,000 bond means $985.",
     "Mutual funds are quoted by net asset value (NAV), set once a day after markets close."],
    ["Every public company's filings are free on SEC EDGAR, the same documents professional analysts use (SEC).",
     "FINRA's free Fund Analyzer compares mutual fund and ETF fees and shows their dollar cost over time (FINRA)."],
    ["In 2021, GameStop's trading volume and price jumped wildly on social-media buzz, far beyond what its financial statements supported; investors who bought at the peak lost heavily.",
     "Companies must file an 8-K within four business days of major events, so news like a CEO resignation appears on EDGAR quickly."],
    ["A high P/E isn't automatically 'bad'; it can mean investors expect fast growth.", "Yield falls when price rises if the dividend stays the same."],
    "\"Before investing, we'd read the company's 10-K on SEC EDGAR, the same source Wall Street analysts use, rather than relying on social media hype.\"",
    [("Where should our client get trustworthy investing information?",
      "Primary sources first. Companies' 10-K and 10-Q filings are free on SEC EDGAR, and Investor.gov and FINRA explain products and check advisors. Morningstar and established financial news add analysis. Social media is where the 2021 GameStop frenzy started, and late buyers lost a lot, so it's a place for ideas, never for decisions. We'd teach the client to check any claim against an official filing."),
     ("How would you explain a P/E ratio to a client?",
      "It's how many dollars investors pay for each dollar of a company's yearly profit. A $50 stock earning $2.50 a share has a P/E of 20. A high P/E means investors expect fast growth, and a low one can mean a bargain or a problem. Comparing a company's P/E with others in its industry is a quick first check on whether it's priced reasonably.")],
    [EDGAR, INV, ["FINRA Fund Analyzer", "https://tools.finra.org/fund_analyzer/"]])

