topic("markets", "Financial markets & the economy", ["Financial Analysis", "Economics"],
      "The types of financial markets, how the Fed and interest rates work, inflation, economic indicators and the business cycle, and financial globalization.")

sub("market-types", "Types of financial markets", ["FI:337"],
    ["Financial markets bring together people who have money and people who need it. The money market trades short-term debt (a year or less), such as Treasury bills, commercial paper, bank CDs and repurchase agreements; it's where governments, banks and companies manage cash. The capital market trades long-term securities, stocks and bonds, that fund growth like new factories or government projects.",
     "Markets are also split by stage. In the primary market, securities are sold for the first time (an IPO or a new bond issue), and the money goes to the issuer. In the secondary market (the NYSE, Nasdaq), investors trade with each other, which gives securities liquidity. Other markets include foreign exchange (currencies), commodities (oil, wheat, gold, often through futures), derivatives (options, futures, swaps), the mortgage market and the insurance market, where risk is bought and sold."],
    ["Money market: short-term, low risk, high liquidity. Capital market: long-term, higher risk and return.",
     "Exchanges (NYSE, Nasdaq) list securities with rules and transparency; over-the-counter (OTC) markets trade directly between dealers.",
     "Investment banks underwrite new issues in the primary market; brokers connect buyers and sellers in the secondary market.",
     "Futures contracts lock in a price today for delivery later; farmers and airlines use them to hedge.",
     "Insurance market: individuals and firms transfer risk to insurers, and insurers transfer it to reinsurers."],
    ["The foreign exchange market is the world's largest, trading about $9.6 trillion a day in April 2025 (Bank for International Settlements Triennial Survey).",
     "Foreign investors hold roughly $9 trillion of U.S. Treasury securities (U.S. Treasury TIC data, 2025)."],
    ["Southwest Airlines famously used fuel futures to lock in cheaper jet fuel before oil prices spiked in the 2000s.",
     "Reddit and Circle went public in 2024-2025 IPOs, raising money in the primary market before trading began on exchanges."],
    ["The money market is about time (short-term), not about 'money market funds' only.", "In the secondary market, the company doesn't receive money from the trade."],
    "\"A CD is a money market tool for short-term safety; an index fund is a capital market tool for long-term growth. Your plan uses both.\"",
    [("Why does it matter to our client which market an investment trades in?",
      "Because the market tells you the time frame, risk and liquidity. Money market investments like Treasury bills are short-term and very safe, right for money needed within a year. Capital market investments like stock funds are for growth over five years or more and can swing a lot. Matching each goal to the right market is the core of the plan: the house down payment goes in the money market and retirement goes in the capital market."),
     ("How does the stock market actually help the economy?",
      "It connects savers with companies that need capital. When a company holds an IPO in the primary market, investors' money funds new products, jobs and factories. The secondary market lets investors sell easily, which makes them willing to invest in the first place. So when our client buys an index fund, they're both building wealth and supplying the capital that keeps businesses growing.")],
    [INV, FED])

sub("fed-rates", "The Federal Reserve and interest rates", ["EC:084", "FI:574"],
    ["The Federal Reserve has a dual mandate from Congress: maximum employment and stable prices, which it defines as 2% inflation over time. Its main tool is the federal funds rate, the rate banks charge each other for overnight loans. The Federal Open Market Committee meets eight times a year to set a target range. When inflation is too high, the Fed raises rates to cool borrowing and spending; when the economy is weak, it cuts rates to encourage them.",
     "Rate changes ripple through the economy. The prime rate moves right away, so credit cards, HELOCs and variable loans change within a billing cycle or two. Savings and CD yields follow. Mortgage rates track the 10-year Treasury yield more than the Fed's rate, so they respond to inflation expectations too. Bond prices move opposite to rates: when rates rise, existing bonds with lower coupons lose value."],
    ["Fed raises rates -> borrowing costs up -> spending and hiring slow -> inflation eases (with a lag of a year or more).",
     "Fed cuts rates -> cheaper loans -> more spending and investment -> growth, and possibly more inflation.",
     "Higher rates help savers and hurt borrowers; they also strengthen the dollar and often pressure stock prices.",
     "The Fed also uses its balance sheet: buying bonds (quantitative easing) pushes long-term rates down; letting them run off (quantitative tightening) does the opposite.",
     "Businesses: higher rates raise the cost of financing expansion and inventory, which changes go/no-go decisions."],
    ["The Fed raised its target range to 3.75%-4.00% on Sept. 16, 2026 and held it there on Oct. 28, 2026 (10-2 vote); its next meeting is Dec. 8-9, 2026 (Federal Reserve).",
     "From March 2022 to July 2023 the Fed raised rates from near zero to 5.25%-5.50%, the fastest increase in four decades (Federal Reserve).",
     "The average 30-year fixed mortgage rate was about 7.11% for the week of Nov. 12, 2026 (Freddie Mac)."],
    ["Under Paul Volcker, the Fed pushed the federal funds rate to about 20% in 1980-81 to break double-digit inflation, causing a recession but ending the inflation of the 1970s.",
     "Rising rates in 2022-23 caused the bond losses that helped sink Silicon Valley Bank."],
    ["The Fed doesn't set mortgage rates directly.", "When rates rise, existing bond prices fall (and vice versa)."],
    "\"Because the Fed raised rates in September, your credit card APR has likely gone up, so paying it off is now a guaranteed 22% return, better than any investment.\"",
    [("How do the Fed's rate decisions affect our client personally?",
      "Directly, through their loans and savings. With the Fed's target at 3.75%-4.00% after the September 2026 hike, credit cards average about 22%, and top savings accounts pay around 4.35%. So we'd pay off variable-rate debt first, lock today's savings yields into a CD ladder, and wait to refinance any fixed-rate mortgage until rates fall. Watching the Fed's December 8-9 meeting tells us which way things are heading."),
     ("Should our client wait for rates to drop before buying a house?",
      "Not necessarily. Nobody can time rates reliably, and home prices can rise while you wait. The better test is whether the payment fits the budget at today's rate, about 7.11% on a 30-year loan in November 2026, with the total housing cost under about 28% of gross income. If rates fall later, they can refinance. If the payment only works at a lower rate, they should wait and save a bigger down payment."),
     ("Why do bond prices fall when interest rates rise?",
      "Because new bonds pay more. If you own a bond paying 3% and new bonds pay 5%, nobody will pay full price for yours, so its price drops until its yield matches. That's what hit bond funds in 2022, when the bond index fell about 13%. For a client, shorter-term bonds and CDs limit that risk, and holding a bond to maturity returns the full face value.")],
    [FED, ["Freddie Mac mortgage rates", "https://www.freddiemac.com/pmms"]])

sub("inflation", "Inflation and its impact", ["EC:083", "FI:574"],
    ["Inflation is a general rise in prices over time, which means each dollar buys less. It's measured mainly by the Consumer Price Index (CPI) from the Bureau of Labor Statistics, which tracks a basket of goods and services, and by the PCE price index from the Bureau of Economic Analysis, the Fed's preferred measure. Core inflation leaves out volatile food and energy prices to show the underlying trend.",
     "Inflation can come from demand-pull (too much spending chasing too few goods), cost-push (higher costs for inputs like energy or wages) or too much money growth. It hurts savers in cash, people on fixed incomes and lenders with fixed-rate loans; it can help borrowers with fixed-rate debt. For businesses, it raises costs, squeezes margins unless prices rise, complicates planning and pushes interest rates up."],
    ["Real return = nominal return - inflation. A 4.35% savings account with 3.7% inflation earns about 0.65% in real terms.",
     "Inflation hedges: stocks over long periods, real estate, Treasury Inflation-Protected Securities (TIPS) and Series I savings bonds.",
     "Businesses respond by repricing, renegotiating supplier contracts, cutting waste, locking in long-term contracts and sometimes 'shrinkflation' (smaller packages at the same price).",
     "Social Security benefits rise each year with a cost-of-living adjustment (COLA) tied to inflation.",
     "Deflation (falling prices) can be worse: people delay purchases, which slows the economy."],
    ["Consumer prices rose 3.7% in the 12 months through September 2026, above the Fed's 2% target (BLS).",
     "CPI inflation peaked at 9.1% in June 2022, the highest in about 40 years (BLS).",
     "Social Security benefits rise 3.6% in January 2027 because of inflation (Social Security Administration, Oct. 2026)."],
    ["Gasoline spiked above $4.50 a gallon in spring 2026, pushing up transportation and shipping costs for businesses.",
     "In 2022-23, many food companies cut package sizes rather than raise prices, a trend the BLS adjusts for in the CPI."],
    ["Inflation reduces purchasing power; it doesn't mean every price rises equally.", "Fixed-rate borrowers gain from unexpected inflation; lenders lose."],
    "\"With inflation at 3.7%, money in a 0.01% savings account is quietly losing about 3.7% of its buying power every year.\"",
    [("How does inflation change your recommendation for our client?",
      "It makes the 'safe' choice less safe. With prices up 3.7% over the year to September 2026, cash earning near zero loses real value every year. So we'd move the emergency fund to a high-yield account earning above inflation, keep long-term money in stock index funds that have historically outpaced inflation, and consider I bonds or TIPS for some protected savings. Inflation is why doing nothing has a cost."),
     ("How should a small business respond to rising costs from inflation?",
      "Protect the margin without losing customers. First, find the true cost increase per product and reprice the items where customers are least price-sensitive. Second, lock in longer supplier contracts and cut waste. Third, communicate value. Coffee shops in 2022, for example, added loyalty perks when they raised prices. Then we'd track gross margin monthly to see if it's working.")],
    [BLS, BEA, ["TreasuryDirect (I bonds)", "https://www.treasurydirect.gov"]])

sub("indicators-cycle", "Economic indicators and the business cycle", ["EC:017", "EC:018", "EC:082", "EC:081"],
    ["The economy moves in a business cycle: expansion (growth, rising jobs and profits), peak, contraction (a recession if it's broad and lasting) and trough, then recovery. The National Bureau of Economic Research officially dates U.S. recessions; a common shortcut is two straight quarters of falling GDP, but the NBER looks at jobs, income, spending and production too. The 2020 recession lasted only two months, the shortest on record.",
     "Gross Domestic Product (GDP) measures the value of all final goods and services produced in a country. It equals consumer spending + business investment + government spending + net exports. Consumer spending is about two-thirds of U.S. GDP, so confidence and jobs matter hugely. The unemployment rate counts people actively looking for work; it lags the cycle. Leading indicators, like the yield curve, jobless claims, building permits and consumer sentiment, try to signal turns ahead of time."],
    ["Leading indicators: stock prices, building permits, new orders, the yield curve, initial jobless claims, consumer expectations.",
     "Coincident indicators: employment, industrial production, personal income.",
     "Lagging indicators: the unemployment rate, the prime rate, inflation.",
     "Business impact: in expansions firms hire and expand; in contractions they cut inventory, delay projects and protect cash.",
     "Types of unemployment: frictional (between jobs), structural (skills mismatch), cyclical (from recessions), seasonal."],
    ["Real GDP grew at a 3.4% annual rate in the third quarter of 2026, up from 2.2% in the second quarter (BEA advance estimate, Oct. 29, 2026).",
     "The unemployment rate was 4.1% in October 2026, as employers added 165,000 jobs (BLS).",
     "Consumer spending makes up about 68% of U.S. GDP (BEA)."],
    ["During the 2008-09 Great Recession, unemployment peaked at 10% in October 2009 (BLS).",
     "Unemployment hit 14.8% in April 2020 during COVID shutdowns, the highest since the data began in 1948 (BLS)."],
    ["Unemployment is a lagging indicator.", "GDP counts final goods only, to avoid double-counting parts."],
    "\"The economy is still expanding: GDP grew 3.4% last quarter and unemployment is 4.1%, which is a good time to build cash reserves before the next slowdown, not after.\"",
    [("How should our client's plan change if a recession hits?",
      "The plan is built for it, so mostly it stays the course. The emergency fund covers three to six months if a job is lost. Long-term investments keep buying through the dip: the Great Recession pushed unemployment to 10% in 2009, but markets fully recovered within a few years. What we'd do is make sure cash reserves are full now, while unemployment is 4.1% and GDP is growing, so a recession is an inconvenience, not a crisis."),
     ("Which economic indicator would you watch to judge this business decision?",
      "For a consumer business, we'd watch consumer spending and confidence, because spending is about two-thirds of U.S. GDP and it leads sales. We'd pair that with the jobs report, since 165,000 jobs added and 4.1% unemployment in October 2026 suggest customers still have income. If those weaken for two or three months in a row, we'd slow the expansion and protect cash. Indicators turn a gut feeling into a decision rule.")],
    [BEA, BLS, ["NBER business cycle dates", "https://www.nber.org/research/business-cycle-dating"]])

sub("globalization", "Financial globalization and exchange rates", ["FI:575", "EC:100", "EC:109"],
    ["Financial globalization is the growing connection of the world's financial markets: money flows across borders in seconds, banks operate in dozens of countries, and investors can buy foreign stocks with a click. It gives businesses access to more capital and investors more diversification, but it also spreads crises quickly. The 2008 U.S. housing crash became a global financial crisis within weeks.",
     "Exchange rates, the price of one currency in another, connect it all. Most major currencies float, set by supply and demand. Rates move with interest-rate differences (higher rates attract foreign money), inflation, trade balances, economic growth and political stability. A strong dollar makes imports and foreign travel cheaper for Americans but makes U.S. exports pricier and shrinks the dollar value of profits earned abroad."],
    ["Strong dollar: good for U.S. importers and tourists; bad for U.S. exporters and multinationals' foreign earnings.",
     "Weak dollar: helps U.S. exporters and tourism to the U.S.; raises import prices.",
     "Businesses hedge currency risk with forward contracts, options or by matching costs and revenue in the same currency.",
     "International institutions: the IMF (stability and emergency lending), World Bank (development), Bank for International Settlements (central banks' bank), WTO (trade rules).",
     "Trade alliances such as USMCA (U.S., Mexico, Canada, 2020) and the EU shape tariffs and rules."],
    ["The foreign exchange market traded about $9.6 trillion a day in April 2025 (BIS Triennial Survey).",
     "The U.S. dollar is on one side of about 89% of all foreign exchange trades (BIS, 2025)."],
    ["The 1997 Asian financial crisis began with Thailand's currency collapse and spread to South Korea, Indonesia and beyond.",
     "Big U.S. multinationals such as Apple and Procter & Gamble often report that a strong dollar cut their reported revenue in some years."],
    ["A stronger dollar hurts U.S. exporters.", "Globalization increases diversification benefits but also contagion risk."],
    "\"About a third of your portfolio could go into international funds: when the dollar weakens, those holdings gain, which adds a second layer of diversification.\"",
    [("Should our client invest internationally?",
      "Yes, a portion, for diversification. U.S. stocks have led recently, but leadership rotates: international stocks beat the U.S. in the 2000s. A total international index fund for 20% to 40% of stock holdings adds thousands of companies in other economies and a hedge against a weaker dollar. The trade-off is currency risk, which is why we keep it a portion, not the whole plan."),
     ("How does a strong dollar affect a U.S. business that sells overseas?",
      "It makes their products more expensive for foreign buyers and shrinks the value of foreign sales when converted to dollars. If a company earns 1 million euros and the euro falls from $1.15 to $1.05, that revenue drops from $1.15 million to $1.05 million without selling one fewer item. The company can hedge with forward contracts, price in local currency or source costs in the same currency. Managing that risk protects the profit plan.")],
    [["Bank for International Settlements", "https://www.bis.org"], ["IMF", "https://www.imf.org"]])

# =====================================================================
topic("credit", "Credit & debt", ["Financial Analysis", "Business Law"],
      "How credit works, credit scores and reports, the true cost of borrowing, getting a loan, debtor-creditor law and getting out of debt.")

sub("credit-basics", "Why credit matters and how to use it wisely", ["FI:002", "FI:071"],
    ["Credit is the ability to buy now and pay later based on a promise to repay, usually with interest. It lets people buy homes, cars and education years before they could pay cash, helps businesses buy inventory and expand, and smooths spending through emergencies. Used well, it builds a credit history that lowers future borrowing costs. Misused, it becomes expensive debt that blocks every other goal.",
     "There are three main types. Revolving credit (credit cards, HELOCs) gives a limit you can borrow against repeatedly. Installment credit (car loans, mortgages, student loans) is a fixed amount repaid in set payments. Open or service credit (utilities, phone bills) is paid in full each month. Credit can be secured by collateral, which the lender can take if you don't pay, or unsecured, which is backed only by your promise and so costs more."],
    ["Lenders judge borrowers by the five Cs: character (track record), capacity (income vs debts), capital (savings and assets), collateral and conditions (loan purpose, economy).",
     "Wise use: pay cards in full, keep utilization under 30% (under 10% is better), never miss a payment, borrow only for things that last longer than the loan.",
     "Truth in Lending Act (1968): lenders must disclose the APR and total finance charge.",
     "Equal Credit Opportunity Act (1974): no discrimination based on race, color, religion, national origin, sex, marital status or age.",
     "Credit CARD Act (2009): 45 days' notice before rate increases, no over-limit fees unless you opt in, limits on cards for people under 21."],
    ["U.S. household debt reached $19.1 trillion in the third quarter of 2026, and credit card balances hit a record $1.29 trillion (New York Fed, Nov. 10, 2026).",
     "Banks charged an average of 22.36% on credit card accounts that paid interest in the third quarter of 2026 (Federal Reserve G.19)."],
    ["Buy now, pay later services like Klarna and Afterpay split purchases into four payments; they're convenient but can make it easy to overspend across several apps.",
     "A first credit card secured by a $300 deposit, or being an authorized user on a parent's card, are common ways teens and young adults start building credit."],
    ["Secured credit is backed by collateral; unsecured is not.", "Credit is not income; it's a promise against future income."],
    "\"Credit is a tool, not extra money. We'd use one card for a few planned bills, pay it in full automatically, and let the on-time history build your score.\"",
    [("How can our client use credit cards responsibly?",
      "Treat the card like a debit card that builds credit. Put a few fixed bills on it, set autopay for the full statement balance, and keep the balance under 30% of the limit. Average card rates are about 22%, so carrying a balance erases any rewards. On-time payment is 35% of a FICO score, so this habit alone builds strong credit within a year or two."),
     ("Is all debt bad?",
      "No. Debt that buys something that grows in value or raises your income, like a reasonable mortgage, a degree with good earning prospects or an equipment loan for a business, can be smart. Debt for things that lose value quickly, especially at 22% on a credit card, is the expensive kind. Our rule: borrow only for things that last longer than the loan, at a payment that fits the budget.")],
    [CFPB, NYFED])

sub("credit-score", "Credit scores and validating your credit history", ["FI:072"],
    ["A credit report is a record of how you've handled credit: accounts, balances, payment history, inquiries, and public records like bankruptcies. Three national credit bureaus, Equifax, Experian and TransUnion, each keep one. A credit score is a three-digit summary of that report that predicts how likely you are to repay. Lenders, landlords, insurers and some employers use reports and scores to make decisions.",
     "Validating your credit history means checking all three reports for errors and fraud and disputing anything wrong. Under the Fair Credit Reporting Act, bureaus must investigate disputes, usually within 30 days, and remove information that can't be verified. Free weekly reports are available at AnnualCreditReport.com, the only official site."],
    ["FICO score: 300-850. 35% payment history, 30% amounts owed (utilization), 15% length of history, 10% new credit, 10% credit mix.",
     "VantageScore, created by the three bureaus, uses the same range with a different formula.",
     "Late payments and collections stay on a report for 7 years; Chapter 7 bankruptcy for 10 years.",
     "Hard inquiries (applying for credit) can lower a score slightly for a year; checking your own credit is a soft inquiry with no effect.",
     "A credit freeze, free at each bureau, stops new accounts being opened in your name."],
    ["Free weekly credit reports from all three bureaus are permanently available at AnnualCreditReport.com (CFPB).",
     "The average FICO score was about 715 in 2025 (FICO).",
     "The bureaus stopped reporting medical debts under $500 and paid medical debts in 2023; a broader CFPB rule to remove medical debt was struck down by a federal court in 2025."],
    ["A good score can save thousands: on a $30,000 car loan, a borrower with excellent credit may pay several percentage points less than one with poor credit, which is thousands of dollars over five years.",
     "In the 2017 Equifax breach, hackers stole personal data on about 147 million Americans, which is why freezes became standard advice (FTC)."],
    ["Checking your own score doesn't hurt it.", "Income is not part of a credit score (but lenders check it separately)."],
    "\"Payment history is 35% of your FICO score, so autopay on every bill is the single best thing you can do for your credit this year.\"",
    [("How can our client build credit from scratch?",
      "Start small and automate on-time payments. Options include a secured card with a $300 deposit, becoming an authorized user on a parent's card with a good history, or a credit-builder loan from a credit union. Use it for one small bill, autopay the full balance and keep usage under 30%. Since payment history is 35% of a FICO score, six to twelve months of this usually produces a solid score."),
     ("What should our client do if they find an error on their credit report?",
      "Dispute it directly with the bureau that shows it, online or in writing, with copies of proof. Under the Fair Credit Reporting Act, the bureau generally has 30 days to investigate and must remove anything it can't verify. Then check the other two bureaus' reports too. If the error is an account they didn't open, it's identity theft, so we'd add a freeze at all three bureaus and file at IdentityTheft.gov.")],
    [ACR, CFPB, ["FTC IdentityTheft.gov", "https://www.identitytheft.gov"]])

sub("cost-credit", "Calculating the cost of credit", ["FI:782"],
    ["The cost of credit is everything you pay beyond the amount borrowed: interest plus fees. The annual percentage rate (APR) states that cost as a yearly rate so loans can be compared, and the Truth in Lending Act requires lenders to show it along with the total finance charge. What drives the cost: the rate, the amount, the length of the loan, fees and how interest is calculated.",
     "Longer loans have lower monthly payments but cost much more in total interest. Credit cards compound interest daily on unpaid balances, and minimum payments are designed to keep you paying for years. A good habit is to always look at the total amount repaid, not just the monthly payment, which is the number salespeople focus on."],
    ["Simple interest: I = P x r x t. $2,000 at 8% for 2 years = $320.",
     "Amortizing loan: each payment covers interest first, then principal; early payments are mostly interest.",
     "$30,000 car loan at 7% for 60 months: about $594 a month and about $5,640 of interest. At 84 months the payment drops but total interest rises to about $8,000.",
     "$5,000 card balance at 22% paying $200 a month: about 34 months and about $1,750 of interest; paying only the minimum can take well over a decade.",
     "Fees add cost: origination fees, balance-transfer fees (often 3%-5%), late fees, annual fees."],
    ["Banks charged an average of 22.36% on credit card accounts that paid interest in the third quarter of 2026 (Federal Reserve G.19).",
     "Federal undergraduate student loans first disbursed from July 1, 2026 to June 30, 2027 carry a fixed 6.52% rate; graduate loans 8.07%; Parent PLUS 9.07% (U.S. Department of Education)."],
    ["By law, every credit card statement shows how long it will take to pay off the balance with only minimum payments, and the total cost (CARD Act).",
     "Car buyers increasingly choose 72- and 84-month loans to lower payments, which raises total interest and the risk of owing more than the car is worth."],
    ["A lower monthly payment can mean a more expensive loan.", "APR includes fees; the interest rate alone doesn't."],
    "\"At 22%, carrying that $5,000 balance costs about $1,100 a year in interest. Paying $200 a month clears it in under three years.\"",
    [("Our client is choosing between a 60-month and an 84-month car loan. What do you recommend?",
      "The 60-month loan, if the payment fits. On $30,000 at 7%, the 60-month payment is about $594 and total interest about $5,640. Stretching to 84 months cuts the payment by roughly $140 but raises interest to about $8,000, and they'd likely owe more than the car is worth for years. If the 60-month payment doesn't fit, a cheaper car is the better fix."),
     ("How would you explain APR to a client?",
      "APR is the yearly price tag of borrowing, including interest and most fees, so you can compare loans fairly. A loan with a 6% rate and a big origination fee might have a 7% APR, while a 6.5% loan with no fees has a 6.5% APR and costs less. The Truth in Lending Act requires lenders to show it. We'd always compare APRs and total repaid, never just the monthly payment.")],
    [CFPB, ["Federal Student Aid interest rates", "https://studentaid.gov/understand-aid/types/loans/interest-rates"]])

sub("loans", "Applying for loans and debtor-creditor relationships", ["FI:625", "BL:071"],
    ["Applying for a consumer loan means proving you can and will repay. Lenders check your credit report and score, verify income and employment (pay stubs, W-2s, tax returns), calculate your debt-to-income ratio and, for secured loans, value the collateral. Shopping around matters: rate quotes for the same type of loan within a short window (about 14 to 45 days, depending on the scoring model) count as one inquiry, and the Loan Estimate form for mortgages makes offers easy to compare.",
     "Once you borrow, you're in a debtor-creditor relationship governed by contract and law. The creditor can charge late fees, report late payments, send the debt to collection and, for secured debt, repossess or foreclose. Unsecured creditors may sue and, with a court judgment, garnish wages. Debtors have protections: the Fair Debt Collection Practices Act bans harassment and false threats, and bankruptcy law offers a last-resort fresh start."],
    ["Mortgage basics: fixed vs adjustable rate, 15 vs 30 years, down payment (20% avoids private mortgage insurance), the 28/36 guideline (housing under 28% of gross income, all debt under 36%).",
     "Cosigning makes you 100% responsible for someone else's debt.",
     "Lien: a creditor's legal claim on property until a debt is paid.",
     "Bankruptcy: Chapter 7 (liquidation, most unsecured debts wiped out), Chapter 13 (3-5 year repayment plan, keep property), Chapter 11 (business reorganization). Most student loans and recent taxes usually survive bankruptcy.",
     "Collectors may not call before 8 a.m. or after 9 p.m., threaten arrest, or discuss your debt with others (FDCPA)."],
    ["The average 30-year fixed mortgage rate was about 7.11% for the week of Nov. 12, 2026 (Freddie Mac).",
     "For 2025 through 2028, interest on loans for new U.S.-assembled personal vehicles is deductible up to $10,000 a year, subject to income limits (IRS, 2025 tax law)."],
    ["Lenders' Loan Estimate and Closing Disclosure forms, required since 2015, let buyers compare mortgage offers line by line (CFPB).",
     "Toys R Us filed Chapter 11 in 2017 hoping to reorganize but ended up liquidating its U.S. stores in 2018 (court filings)."],
    ["Chapter 7 liquidates; Chapter 13 repays over time.", "A cosigner is fully liable, not just a backup reference."],
    "\"Before you apply, we'll pull your free credit reports, fix any errors, and get three quotes within two weeks so they count as a single inquiry.\"",
    [("How can our client get the best rate on their first car or home loan?",
      "Prepare before applying. Check all three credit reports for errors, pay card balances below 30% of their limits, and gather pay stubs and tax returns. Then get pre-approved by two or three lenders, including a credit union, within about two weeks so the inquiries count as one. Even half a percentage point on a $300,000 mortgage saves tens of thousands over 30 years. Comparing APRs is the cheapest win in the whole plan."),
     ("A friend asked our client to cosign a loan. What would you advise?",
      "Only do it if they're willing and able to pay the whole loan themselves. A cosigner is 100% legally responsible: if the friend misses payments, the lender comes to the cosigner, and the late payments hit their credit report too. A safer way to help is a gift they can afford to lose, or helping the friend build credit with a secured card. Protecting the client's credit protects every goal in their plan.")],
    [CFPB, ["CFPB: buying a house", "https://www.consumerfinance.gov/owning-a-home/"]])

sub("debt-control", "Controlling and paying off debt", ["FI:568"],
    ["Debt is under control when payments fit comfortably in the budget, balances are falling and there's an emergency fund so new surprises don't add more. Warning signs include paying only minimums, using one card to pay another, being turned down for credit, collection calls, or not knowing how much you owe. The first step is always a full list: every debt, balance, rate and minimum payment.",
     "Two payoff methods work. The avalanche method pays minimums on everything and puts extra money toward the highest-rate debt first, which saves the most interest. The snowball method attacks the smallest balance first for quick wins, which keeps some people motivated. Tools include balance-transfer cards (0% for a period, usually with a 3%-5% fee), consolidation loans at a lower rate, nonprofit credit counseling and, as a last resort, bankruptcy."],
    ["Debt-to-income (DTI) = monthly debt payments / gross monthly income. Under 36% is a common healthy guideline; many mortgage lenders cap it around 43%-45%.",
     "Avalanche = highest APR first (mathematically best). Snowball = smallest balance first (behaviorally motivating).",
     "Nonprofit credit counselors (NFCC members) can set up a debt management plan with lower rates; beware for-profit 'debt settlement' firms that charge big fees and damage credit.",
     "Call the lender before missing a payment; many offer hardship programs.",
     "Stop the leak: freeze new card spending while paying off."],
    ["About 6.87% of credit card balances became 90 or more days delinquent (annualized) in the third quarter of 2026, down from 7.05% a year earlier (New York Fed).",
     "Credit card balances reached a record $1.29 trillion in the third quarter of 2026 (New York Fed)."],
    ["Federal student loan borrowers saw delinquencies hit credit reports again in 2025 after the pandemic pause ended, and many scores fell sharply (New York Fed).",
     "The CFPB has fined debt-settlement companies for charging fees before settling any debts, which is illegal under the FTC's Telemarketing Sales Rule."],
    ["Debt settlement and credit counseling are different; counseling usually doesn't hurt credit like settlement does.", "Avalanche saves more interest; snowball builds motivation."],
    "\"We'd use the avalanche method: minimums on everything, and every extra dollar to the 24% card first. That saves the most interest, and we'll celebrate each payoff.\"",
    [("Our client has three credit cards and a car loan. How do they get out of debt?",
      "First, list every balance and rate and stop adding new charges. Second, build a $1,000 starter emergency fund so surprises don't go on a card. Third, pay minimums on everything and put every extra dollar toward the highest-rate card, the avalanche method, then roll that payment to the next one. If they qualify, a 0% balance-transfer card can cut the interest while they pay. We'd track total debt monthly so they see it shrinking."),
     ("Should our client use a debt settlement company they saw advertised?",
      "We'd be very cautious. Settlement companies often tell clients to stop paying, which brings late fees, collection calls and serious credit damage, and they charge large fees. Forgiven debt over $600 can also be taxable income. A nonprofit credit counselor that's a member of the NFCC can usually negotiate lower rates through a debt management plan without those risks. That protects the client's credit while they pay it off.")],
    [CFPB, NYFED, ["NFCC nonprofit credit counseling", "https://www.nfcc.org"]])
